{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310836,"nonce":"5Ls8b/FEbLO0AbQ8+Iw2+OzVZgs=","title":"Iris Xe iGPU for prefill: MEASURED, not assumed. Register-tiled WGSL matmul 98-\u003e379 GF/s (3.0-3.6x), now ~3.2x the CPU Q4 prefill path. GPU ...","message":"2026-08-03. User raised GPU explicitly (standing rule\n[[feedback_gpu_not_a_cpu_lever]] says never without that), then: **\"We need to\nmeasure before we assume.\"** Right call — my prior reasoning was wrong twice.\n\nNUC i5-1250P has **Iris Xe (Alder Lake-P GT2, 8086:46a6)**, `/dev/dri/renderD128`\nreadable over ssh via ACL (`user:raikage:rw-`, NOT group membership), and an\n**`intel_icd.json` Vulkan ICD + libvulkan.so.1**. No OpenCL, no Level Zero —\nirrelevant, wgpu goes via Vulkan. Build: `cargo build -p rayzor-gpu\n--features webgpu-backend`. (Also: sudo IS available on the NUC; an earlier\nnote claiming otherwise was wrong.)\n\nBench: `~/gpubench` on the NUC — F32 GEMM at Mistral-7B prefill shapes, GPU vs\nthe CPU tensor plugin, both plugins loaded together.\n\n| shape | simple 16x16 | register-tiled |\n|---|---|---|\n| attn-proj 653x4096x4096 | 98.3 | **349.2 GF/s** |\n| ffn-gate 653x4096x14336 | 125.0 | **378.8 GF/s** |\n| warmup 64x256x256 | 1.9 | **1.9** (CPU 22.5 — GPU LOSES) |\n\nBit-identical to the simple kernel on every shape (checksums exact).\n\nFirst pass showed \"13.9x faster than CPU\". **That CPU column was the tensor\nplugin's F32 matmul, which prefill NEVER RUNS.** Real prefill is the Q4_K band\nkernel: 9.34 TFLOP / 80.99s TTFT = **115.3 GF/s effective**. Against that the\nnaive GPU kernel was at PARITY, not 14x. **Always convert a microbench to the\nunits of the real workload before believing a ratio.**\nTiled vs the true baseline: **~3.2x**.\n\n16x16 tile = one output per thread = 1 FMA per 2 SLM loads -\u003e ALUs starve at\n7% of the ~1.79 TFLOPS peak. Register tiling: each thread owns TM x TN of C,\nso a k-step loads TM+TN and issues TM*TN FMAs.\n\nSwept on the ffn-gate shape, 3 runs each (GF/s):\n    64x128 BK16 4x8   382 380 380   \u003c- DEFAULT\n    64x64  BK32 4x4   385 377 377   (tied)\n    128x64 BK16 8x4   365 367 363\n    64x64  BK16 4x4   325           (first guess, -15%)\n    128x128 BK16 8x8   24           \u003c- SPILLS 15x\n\n**TM*TN \u003c= 32. 8x8 = 64 accumulators spills catastrophically** — the SAME\nregister-pressure wall that made the CPU 4-\u003e8 tile widening lose\n([[perf_attention_split_measured_ffn_is_the_lever]]). Sweepable via\nRZG_BM/BN/BK/TM/TN; `RZG_MATMUL=simple` restores the old kernel for A/B in one\nbinary.\n\nStill only ~21% of FP32 peak. **Untried: vec4 loads, f16 (shader-f16 doubles\nXe throughput), Q4-aware kernel (no dequant round-trip).**\n\nIt had never been run against real work. All were invisible until load:\n1. No `rayzor_plugin_abi_version` export -\u003e loader refused it as ABI v0.\n2. `src/native_libs.rs` accepted fewer entry-point names than\n   `compiler/src/rpkg/install.rs` (which already knew `rayzor_gpu_plugin_init`).\n3. **`NativeMethodDesc::param_types` was `[u8; 8]` but `_param_array!`'s\n   9-arg arm emitted 8 while `_param_count!` said 9** -\u003e `rope` (9 params) made\n   the loader slice `[..9]` of a length-8 array and PANIC. Widened to\n   `MAX_PARAMS = 16`, replaced the hand-unrolled macro with ONE generic arm\n   (cannot drift from the count), ABI_VERSION 1 -\u003e 2.\n4. **All 41 compute bindings named nonexistent symbols** —\n   `@:native(\"gpu_compute_createBuffer\")` vs exported\n   `rayzor_gpu_compute_create_buffer`. Convention (from Tensor/SIMD): class\n   `@:native(\"rayzor::gpu::X\")` \u003c-\u003e descriptor `\"rayzor_gpu_X\"`, and each\n   method's `@:native` IS the exported symbol. Also caught `BindGroup.destroy`\n   bound to the LAYOUT destructor.\n\nPlus `wgpu::Limits::default()` caps a storage binding at 128 MiB \u003c one 7B FFN\nweight (224 MiB) -\u003e validation failure before any kernel ran. Use\n`adapter.limits()`.\n\n- **Size gate**: small shapes lose 12x. Model it on the AMX gate in\n  `Q4Matmul.hx` (batch \u003e= N \u0026\u0026 scheme \u0026\u0026 contiguous, CPU fallback, census\n  counts a PLATFORM escape).\n- Q4_K weights must reach the GPU. Dequant-to-f16 costs bandwidth on a SHARED\n  DRAM bus; a Q4-aware WGSL kernel avoids the round-trip entirely.\n- CPU+iGPU co-execution is UNMEASURED (shared bandwidth + 28W shared power).\n- Mac/Metal path (`msl_matmul.rs`) has the SAME naive 16x16 kernel and did not\n  get this treatment.\n\nAgainst a design spec proposing single-copy weights on UMA (one physical copy\naddressed by CPU and GPU). Spec's Path A = allocate from the\n`DEVICE_LOCAL|HOST_VISIBLE|HOST_COHERENT` heap and load into the mapping;\nPath B = import the mmap'd GGUF via `VK_EXT_external_memory_host`.\n\n**Both are unreachable through wgpu.** No API selects a memory type; the\nextension is not exposed. Tested the one thing wgpu *does* offer —\n`create_buffer(mapped_at_creation:true)` + write + unmap, vs the existing\n`create_buffer_init` (staging):\n\n| | upload 246 MB | system RAM consumed (1280 MiB payload) |\n|---|---|---|\n| staging | 0.0545 s | 2143 MiB |\n| mapped_at_creation | 0.0545 s | 2228 MiB |\n\n**Identical time, no memory saved (mapped marginally WORSE).** wgpu allocates a\ndevice buffer either way. REVERTED — no measured win. To get single-copy we\nmust drop below wgpu (wgpu-hal escape or a native Vulkan path in rayzor-gpu).\n\nFirst attempt measured process RSS: 1459 vs 1451 MiB for a 1280 MiB payload —\ni.e. the GPU buffer appeared to cost NOTHING. **i915 backs buffers with GEM\nobjects that are not counted in process RSS but do consume system RAM.** Use\n`MemAvailable` delta instead; it showed the real ~2.1 GiB. A GPU allocation\nthat looks free in RSS is not free.\n\n- CONFIRMED: decode gains nothing from the iGPU (we measured 10 GF/s GPU vs\n  115 GF/s CPU Q4 at m=1); phase-split prefill=GPU/decode=CPU; never run both\n  concurrently (one DRAM controller); capacity is the binding constraint.\n- CORRECTED: spec predicts 3-5 tok/s decode for 7-8B Q4. **Measured 7.71\n  (rayzor) / 7.92 (llama.cpp)** on this box — ~2x pessimistic.\n- CORRECTED: spec cites 8-12 tok/s prefill for Iris Xe. Our CPU-only prefill is\n  8.06, so that figure describes the double-copy stack, not the ceiling. Tiled\n  kernel implies ~25 tok/s.\n- FRAMING: spec calls this \"capacity engineering, not performance\". For RAYZOR\n  it is both — our prefill is 3.8x behind llama.cpp while decode is at parity,\n  so GPU prefill attacks exactly the gap we have.\n- Resolved its open questions: Q-2 dual-channel (41.8 GB/s measured \u003e 25.6\n  single-channel ceiling); Q-3 PCI 8086:46A6 = ADL-P **GT2** (80 EU, not 32);\n  Q-6 **i915** owns 0000:00:02.0 (both i915 and xe modules loaded).\n- UNDER-WEIGHTED by the spec: `int dot: 1` (DP4A) is the keystone — it is what\n  makes a Q4/Q8-aware GPU kernel efficient instead of a dequant-to-f32 detour.\n- Our 2 GiB `max_buffer_size` REINFORCES its M-5: one allocation with buffer\n  views at offsets is how 4.07 GiB of weights gets past a 2 GiB per-buffer cap.\n\nI concluded \"we need a Vulkan path under wgpu\" for single-copy weights. **Wrong,\nand the user's \"why?\" exposed it.** I had generalised the spec's requirement\nwithout checking whether our workload has it.\n\nThe spec optimises for WHOLE-MODEL GPU RESIDENCY. We never need that: decode\nstays on CPU (measured), and prefill is a SEQUENCE OF INDEPENDENT MATMULS —\neach weight touched exactly once. The GPU needs ONE weight at a time:\nper-weight scratch is 235 MB (f32) or **33 MB (Q4_K)**, not 4.07 GiB. That fits\nwgpu today, no new backend. (Also: a Vulkan backend would sit BESIDE wgpu in\n`gpu/src/backend.rs`'s enum, not \"under\" it — my wording was wrong too.)\n\nSingle-copy only pays for: models that do not fit at all (13B+), or removing\nthe streaming cost. KV sharing (spec T2) is 272 MB @ q8_0/4k = ~60 ms to copy\nonce per request — does not justify a backend either.\n\nMeasured on a REAL Mistral Q4_K FFN weight (14336x4096, batch 653), full\nper-matmul offload:\n\n| stage | ms | share |\n|---|---|---|\n| dequant Q4-\u003ef32 (CPU) | 87.9 | 19% |\n| upload | 83.6 | 18% |\n| **GEMM** | **261.1** | **56%** |\n| readback | 33.7 | 7% |\n| TOTAL | 466.3 | -\u003e **164.5 GF/s** |\n\n**vs CPU 115.3 GF/s = 1.43x, NOT the 3.2x the GEMM-only microbench showed.**\nProjected TTFT 81.0s -\u003e 56.8s.\n\nProjections from the same measurement:\n- **+ Q4-aware kernel** (no dequant; upload 33 MB not 235 MB): 250 GF/s =\n  **2.17x**, TTFT 37.3s\n- **+ activations resident across a layer** (no per-matmul readback):\n  281 GF/s = **2.44x**, TTFT 33.2s\n\n**CONCLUSION: do NOT wire the naive path.** The Q4-aware WGSL kernel is now a\nPREREQUISITE, not an optimisation — it converts 1.43x into 2.17x and drops the\nper-weight scratch from 235 MB to 33 MB. `int dot: 1` (DP4A) is what makes it\nefficient.\n\n**LESSON: a kernel microbenchmark measured 56% of the real chain.** Always\nmeasure the whole path — dequant, transfer, compute, readback — before\nbelieving a kernel speedup translates.\n\n`rayzor_gpu_compute_matmul_q4k` reads Q4_K_M blocks in-shader; weights are\nuploaded as raw bytes via `rayzor_gpu_compute_buffer_from_bytes`. A 144-byte\nblock is 4-aligned = exactly 36 u32 words; **`unpack2x16float` decodes the f16\nd/dmin pair natively** (core WGSL, no f16 extension needed). BK=32 = one\nsub-block, so a k-tile shares one (block, j) per column and the header is\ndecoded ONCE per column per tile. Scale/min unpack + nibble grouping mirror\n`q4DotMA4`.\n\nMistral FFN gate 14336x4096, batch 653:\n\n| route | breakdown | total | GF/s | vs CPU 115.3 |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| f32 | dq 89.7 + up 61.4 + gemm 293.9 | 445.0 ms | 172 | 1.49x |\n| **q4k** | **up 4.8 + gemm 226.0** | **230.8 ms** | **332** | **2.88x** |\n\nUpload -12.7x (235 MB -\u003e 31 MiB). **The GEMM itself got FASTER (294-\u003e226 ms)**\n— B-tile loads pull 7x less DRAM traffic, so the kernel was partly\nbandwidth-bound. Projected TTFT 81.0s -\u003e 28.1s. Beat the 2.17x projection.\n\nComparing against `gpu.matmul` showed sums agreeing to **5e-6** while ~EVERY\nelement was wrong (9346389 of 9361408 outliers). `gpu.matmul` computes\n`A x B[k,n]` UNTRANSPOSED; a weight is `[n,K]`. Both sum the SAME BUFFER, so\ntotals match under a transpose. **An all-ones activation hides it completely**\n(every row identical). Correct oracle = CPU `dequant` + `matmulT` with RANDOM\nactivations: then 0/9361408 outliers, max |diff| 3.2e-6.\n**A matching SUM is not a correctness check. Compare elementwise, with random\ninputs, against an oracle with the same transpose semantics.**\n\nKernel is correct and fast but nue still calls the CPU path. To wire:\n1. size gate (small shapes lose ~12x) modelled on the AMX gate in Q4Matmul.hx\n2. per-weight upload reuse (or upload-once if a weight is used repeatedly)\n3. activations resident across a layer would remove the readback (7%)\n\nWired the Q4_K kernel into `Q4Matmul.matmul()` behind `#if gpu` +\n`NUE_GPU_PREFILL=1` + a batch gate, and ran real Mistral-7B prefill (653-token\nprompt) on the NUC.\n\n| run | exit | ttft |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | 0 | 67.48 s |\n| 2 | **139 SIGSEGV** in `[gen] streaming...` | — |\n| 3 | 0 | 66.97 s |\n\nOutput is COHERENT when it survives (\"You are correct that a compiler is a\").\n**TTFT 81.0 -\u003e ~67 s = 1.21x.** nue wiring REVERTED; the kernel commits stay.\n\nThe 2.88x extrapolated ONE shape's throughput (the 14336x4096 FFN weight) to\nall 9.34 TFLOP. Production differs:\n- **readback per matmul** (~34 ms x 224 = ~7.6 s) — excluded from the projection\n- **activation upload per matmul**, on top of the weight upload\n- **buffer create/free churn**: 224 alloc+free cycles of 31-235 MiB per prefill\n- **small shapes**: k/v projections are 4096x1024, far off the FFN's efficiency\n\n**Third time this session a microbench overstated the real path** (kernel-only\n3.2x -\u003e chain 1.43x -\u003e routed 1.21x). Extrapolating one favourable shape across\na heterogeneous workload is the recurring error.\n\nIntermittent (1/3), and it does NOT reproduce under gdb (ran clean there), so\nit is timing-dependent. Suspects, untested:\n1. `WgpuContext.pending` is a `RefCell` (added by the dispatch-batching commit\n   8e507b0c) — **not Sync**. If any caller touches the GPU from two threads it\n   is UB.\n2. Resource lifetime under LAZY submission: `dims_buf` is a local in\n   `matmul_q4k` and drops when the function returns, while the encoded pass\n   may not be submitted yet. The bind group is retained, which *should* keep\n   it alive via wgpu-core Arcs — but this is exactly the invariant lazy\n   submission changed, and the f32 path has the same shape.\n3. 224 large alloc/free cycles per prefill stressing the i915 allocator.\n\n**Next step is the crash, not more tuning.** Cheapest discriminator: force\n`flush()` immediately after every dispatch (restoring eager submission) and see\nif the segfault disappears — that isolates (2) from (1)/(3) in one run.\n\nTwo clean negative results (branch `debug/gpu-prefill-routing`, not merged):\n\n**1. Not memory.** Timestamped MemAvailable across a failing run:\n`t=21s 5299 -\u003e t=24s 5053 -\u003e t=27s 4994 MiB (CRASH) -\u003e t=30s 5872 (process gone)`.\n**~5 GiB free at the moment of death.** Starvation hypothesis dead.\n\n**2. Not lazy submission.** `RZG_EAGER=1` restores per-dispatch submit+wait:\n| | run1 | run2 | run3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| LAZY | SIGSEGV | OK ttft 66.4 | SIGSEGV |\n| EAGER | SIGSEGV | OK ttft 68.6 | **HANG (900s timeout)** |\n1/3 success either way, so `dims_buf`-drops-before-submit is dead too. Byte\nsizing also checks out: `n*(K\u003e\u003e8)*144` == `numel*144/256`, matching\nRayzorQTensor's layout, so it is not an OOB read from my sizing either.\n\n**3. What is left: SEGFAULTS *AND* HANGS, intermittent, under sustained load,\nwith numerically CORRECT output whenever it completes.** That is not a\nmemory-safety signature — it is driver instability, and **the design spec\npredicted exactly this** (\"Threat 1: Intel iGPU Vulkan backends have a\ndocumented history... Alder Lake specifically... V-2/V-5 are the gate\").\nI dismissed it earlier on the strength of exact matmul checksums. Those are\nstill exact — the arithmetic is right every time it finishes. **I never ran\nV-5: the same work repeated many times.** 224 dispatches per prefill is far\nmore sustained load than any correctness test I ran.\n\nBuild llama.cpp with `-DGGML_VULKAN=ON` on the NUC and run a comparable\nsustained load. If it is also unstable, the platform is at fault and no amount\nof work on our side fixes it. If it is stable, the bug is ours and llama.cpp's\nVulkan backend shows what we are doing differently. **Cheaper than continuing\nto bisect our own code, and it decides whether to continue at all.**\n\nKernel commits (30402460, ef978120, 8e507b0c, 988733b3, 17770f07, a833c7ba)\nare all on main and independently verified. Routing is NOT merged. Measured\n1.21x end-to-end when it survives, vs the kernel's isolated 2.88x.\n\n`rayzor_gpu_compute_to_tensor` hand-rolled RayzorTensor's layout and malloc'd\n**48 bytes**. The struct is **64**: `refcount` @48 and `parent` @56 both sat\nPAST THE END of the allocation. `rayzor_tensor_free` read a garbage refcount,\nfollowed a garbage `parent`, recursed into free() -\u003e SIGSEGV (3 nested\n`rayzor_tensor_free` frames in the backtrace).\n\nInvisible for as long as it existed because it only fires when something FREES\na to_tensor result — the GPU tests call `sum()` and stop. Routing was the first\ncode to hand one to the runtime's normal lifecycle. Benign-or-not depended on\nallocator leftovers, hence intermittent (1-2 of 3), never reproducible under\ngdb, and wandering between SIGSEGV and SIGILL.\n\n**FIX: delete the duplicated layout, do not correct the constant.** to_tensor\nnow calls `rayzor_tensor_uninit` via `dlsym` (rayzor-gpu does not link\nrayzor-tensors; the host exports its symbols) and REFUSES if absent. Hard-coding\n64 would have re-armed the trap for the next field added to RayzorTensor.\n**Verified 4/4 clean, ttft 66.7-69.0s**, full cache sweep + --no-cache per run.\n\ngdb never reproduced it; core dumps unavailable (core_pattern -\u003e apport,\n`ulimit -c` 0, both need root). **`RZG_CRASH_TRACE=1`: an in-process\nSIGSEGV/SIGBUS/SIGILL handler printing `backtrace_symbols_fd` to stderr.** One\nrun, one stack, done. Committed and env-gated.\n\nWrong answers, in order, each from reasoning about an exit code instead of\nlooking: (1) memory starvation — refuted, ~5 GiB free at the fault;\n(2) lazy submission / `dims_buf` lifetime — refuted, crash survives RZG_EAGER;\n(3) driver instability — I was one step from a llama.cpp-Vulkan bake-off;\n(4) **a fabricated compiler \"bare-name `fromString`\" bug** — pure cache\nartefact, retracted. Discriminators finally ran clean: base 2/3, eager 2/3,\nworkers=1 1/3 — ALL crash, so submission timing and pool contention were never\ninvolved.\n\n**GET A BACKTRACE BEFORE FORMING A THEORY.** Four hypotheses and ~15 runs cost\nmore than the one signal handler that answered it.\n\n**THIS SECTION IS WRONG — the controls were CACHE-CONTAMINATED.** With full\ncache sweeps + `--no-cache` on every arm: routing OFF is 4/4 CLEAN with zero\ntrap stubs, routing ON crashed. The trap stubs, the `fromString` arity\nmismatch and the \"bare-name resolution\" story were all stale-cache artefacts.\nThe real cause is the 48-vs-64-byte wrapper above. Kept as a record of the\nfalse trail.\n\nThe failing runs print it plainly (I never read the log, only the signal number):\n```\nWarning: [W0020] cannot compile `nue.chat.Conversation.ask`; runtime calls will SIGILL.\nIncorrect number of arguments passed to called function!\n  %35 = call i64 @fromString(i64 0, ptr %29, ptr %34, ptr %2)\n```\n4 trap stubs installed: `BertArch.build`, `BertArch.takeNormAny`,\n`Conversation.ask`, `Conversation.nextPrompt`. The last two ARE the generation\npath — hence death in `[gen] streaming...`.\n\n**Root cause: bare-name method resolution.** Call site is\n`Architectures.fromString(...)` (1 arg, `nue/nue/model/Architecture.hx:33`).\nCompeting `fromString` declarations exist in `Date.hx:182` and\n`sys/ssl/Certificate.hx:30`. The compiler picked by NAME, emitted a 4-arg call\nto a 1-arg function, LLVM verify rejected it, and the function became a trap\nstub. Enlarging the classpath (adding `gpu/haxe`) just widened the candidate\nset. **This is exactly [[feedback_fqn_first_resolution]]: \"bare-name lookup IS\nthe defect.\"** Same family as [[bugs_new_module_static_only_sigsegv]] —\nmodule-count-sensitive resolution.\n\nI read `EXIT=139` / `EXIT=132` and reasoned about what *could* produce them\nacross ~6 experiments (memory timeline, eager-vs-lazy submission, byte-size\naudit, cross-thread probe, reshape lifetimes, gdb). **The program printed\n`runtime calls will SIGILL` with the offending function NAMED on every single\nfailing run.** `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1` would have named it in one run\n([[bugs_trap_stub_cascade]]).\n**READ THE PROGRAM'S OWN OUTPUT BEFORE THEORISING ABOUT A SIGNAL NUMBER.**\nCorollary: SIGILL in rayzor means TRAP STUB until proven otherwise — it is the\ndocumented mechanism, not a wild pointer.\n\nAlso: I was one step from a llama.cpp-Vulkan bake-off to blame the driver. The\nsignal number sent me to the platform; the log line was in every run.\n\nKernel + routing are sound: coherent output, TTFT 81.0 -\u003e ~67s (1.21x) whenever\nthe trap stub is not hit. **The blocker is a compiler fix (FQN-first\nresolution), not GPU work.** Once `fromString` resolves correctly the routing\ncan be re-measured honestly.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_igpu_prefill_alderlake` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}